.TH "BCR" "1" "" "" "Breadcrumb SDD"
.SH "NAME"
.PP
bcr \- audit the breadcrumbs of a repository
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr audit\-report \-\-html [\-o FILE]
bcr check
bcr extract [FILE...]
bcr verify
bcr verdict [ID|PATH|\-]...
bcr links [\-r] [ID|PATH|\-]...
bcr new TYPE TITLE [PARENT|\-]...
bcr spec
bcr init [\-a FILE]
.fi
.RE
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBbcr\fR is the toolkit of Breadcrumb SDD. It reads the breadcrumbs of a repository, checks the claims of its Tasks against the repository's files, and reports what it finds. Run it from the root of the repository.
.PP
This document is the contract of the interface of \fBbcr\fR: its commands, flags, inputs, outputs and exit codes. The rules of the method are in the breadcrumbs of the Breadcrumb SDD repository, and this document points to the one that holds each rule (TD\-0018).
.PP
Every command follows the same conventions:
.IP \(bu 2
It is run as \fBbcr COMMAND [FLAGS] [OPERANDS]\fR. Flags come before operands; the first operand, or \fB\-\-\fR, ends them (TD\-0020).
.IP \(bu 2
Flags are in getopt style: a short flag is one letter, such as \fB\-a\fR, and short flags can be combined, as in \fB\-ab\fR; a long flag is a name, such as \fB\-\-html\fR. A flag's value follows it: \fB\-x VALUE\fR, \fB\-xVALUE\fR, \fB\-\-name=VALUE\fR or \fB\-\-name VALUE\fR (RQ\-0011).
.IP \(bu 2
Results go to standard output, and nothing else does. Messages go to standard error and start with \fBbcr: \fR. A usage error is followed by the usage line (RQ\-0009, TD\-0020).
.IP \(bu 2
The exit status says whether the command found something wrong (see EXIT STATUS).
.IP \(bu 2
A command that takes breadcrumbs as operands takes an id, such as \fBTK\-0001\fR, a path relative to the repository root, such as \fBbreadcrumbs/TK\-0001.md\fR, or \fB\-\fR, which reads one breadcrumb per line of standard input, named by the line's first tab\-separated field. With no operands, it takes every breadcrumb, in order of path. An operand that names no breadcrumb is written to standard error, the others are still taken, and the command exits with 2 (TD\-0026, RQ\-0012, RQ\-0025).
.PP
Which files are breadcrumbs: TD\-0013. The kinds of breadcrumb: TD\-0001, TD\-0003, TD\-0004 and TD\-0011. How a breadcrumb names its parents: TD\-0005.
.PP
The rules of the method are gathered in its specification, breadcrumb\-sdd.md, which \fBbcr spec\fR prints (TD\-0028).
.SH "COMMANDS"
.SS "audit\-report"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr audit\-report \-\-html [\-o FILE]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Audits the breadcrumbs and writes the result as an HTML report (RQ\-0001).
.PP
Flags:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-html\fR: write the report as HTML. It is required, and HTML is the only format.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-o FILE\fR, \fB\-\-output FILE\fR: write the report to \fBFILE\fR instead of \fBaudit\-report.html\fR, replacing any file there. A relative \fBFILE\fR is taken from the current directory. \fB\-o \-\fR writes the report to standard output (RQ\-0017, RQ\-0018).
.PP
Operands: none.
.PP
Standard input: not read.
.PP
Output: without \fB\-o\fR, the report is written to \fBaudit\-report.html\fR in the current directory, replacing any file there (RQ\-0019). When the report is written to a file, standard output gets one line, the absolute path of the report. With \fB\-o \-\fR, standard output gets the report, and no file is written.
.PP
The report draws the breadcrumbs as a graph (RQ\-0002), each with its properties (RQ\-0003) and an arrow to each of its parents (RQ\-0004), colored by its verdict (RQ\-0005). How a verdict is reached: TD\-0012 for a claim, RQ\-0007 for a Task, and RQ\-0006 and RQ\-0008 for the breadcrumbs above it. The report lists the same problems as \fBbcr check\fR, each with its file and line.
.PP
Exit status:
.IP \(bu 2
0: the report is written, and no breadcrumb is Refuted or has a problem.
.IP \(bu 2
1: the report is written, and a breadcrumb is Refuted or has a problem.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, or there is no \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR directory in the current directory, or the report could not be written, such as to a directory that does not exist. No report is written.
.SS "check"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr check
.fi
.RE
.PP
Checks the syntax of the breadcrumbs and prints each problem it finds (RQ\-0020).
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands: none. Every breadcrumb is checked.
.PP
Standard input: not read.
.PP
Output: one line on standard output for each problem, in order of path and then of line:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
PATH:LINE: MESSAGE
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBPATH\fR is relative to the repository root, and \fBLINE\fR is counted from 1 (TD\-0020). What is a problem: a first line that is not a title (TD\-0013), a \fBParent:\fR line that is not a link or links to no breadcrumb (TD\-0005, RQ\-0022), and a list item under a Task's \fB## Claims\fR heading that is not a claim (TD\-0011, TD\-0012). A claim that does not hold is not a problem; \fBbcr audit\-report\fR shows it in the Task's verdict.
.PP
Standard error gets a warning for each file in \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR that is not a breadcrumb (RQ\-0023):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr: warning: PATH is not a breadcrumb (TD\-0013)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Exit status (RQ\-0021):
.IP \(bu 2
0: no problem was found. Warnings do not change it.
.IP \(bu 2
1: a problem was found.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, or there is no \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR directory in the current directory.
.SS "extract"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract [FILE...]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reads the breadcrumb of each file it is given, or of each file \fB.breadcrumbs\fR names, from the file's front matter, and prints it as records other programs can use.
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands: the files to read, in the order given. Which files to read is the caller's choice, for example with \fBgit ls\-files\fR or \fBfind\fR; \fBbcr extract\fR does not check the name or the path of a file. Given operands, \fB.breadcrumbs\fR is not read.
.PP
With no operand, \fBbcr extract\fR reads every file that \fB.breadcrumbs\fR, in the current directory, names, in order of path compared as bytes. Run it from the root of the repository. The files are found from the current directory: a directory named \fB.git\fR is not entered, symbolic links are not followed, and only regular files are read.
.PP
\fB.breadcrumbs\fR holds one pattern on each line. Blank lines, and lines that start with \fB#\fR, are ignored:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# the decisions, the specs and the tasks
docs/adrs/*.md
specs/*/spec.md
tasks/*.md
!tasks/README.md
.fi
.RE
.PP
A pattern is matched against the whole path of a file from the current directory, with \fB/\fR between its parts. \fB*\fR matches any characters except \fB/\fR, \fB?\fR one character except \fB/\fR, and \fB[...]\fR one character from a set. Unlike in \fB.gitignore\fR, a pattern with no \fB/\fR names only files at the root: \fB*.md\fR does not name \fBdocs/bcr.md\fR. A pattern that starts with \fB!\fR excludes the files it matches. When several lines match a file, the last one decides; a file no line matches is not read.
.PP
A pattern may not use \fB**\fR, start or end with \fB/\fR, be empty after \fB!\fR, or be malformed, such as with a \fB[\fR that does not close. Such a line is a problem, written to standard error as \fB.breadcrumbs:LINE: MESSAGE\fR and to standard output as a \fBproblem\fR record, and then no file is read.
.PP
Standard input: not read.
.PP
Output: for a file with a valid breadcrumb, one \fBbreadcrumb\fR record on standard output, then one \fBlink\fR record for each entry of its \fBlinks\fR, then one \fBclaim\fR record for each valid entry of its \fBclaims\fR, each in the order written. Fields are separated by a tab:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
breadcrumb	ID	TYPE	PATH	LINE
link	ID	VERB	OBJECT	PATH	LINE
claim	ID	KIND	TARGET	ARGUMENT	PATH	LINE
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBPATH\fR is the file as given, or, with no operand, its path from the current directory, with \fB/\fR between its parts. \fBLINE\fR is the line of the file where the record was written, counted from 1: for a \fBbreadcrumb\fR record, the line of its \fBid\fR; for a \fBlink\fR record, the line of its entry. In a \fBlink\fR record, \fBID\fR is the id of the breadcrumb the link belongs to, and \fBVERB\fR and \fBOBJECT\fR are the two words of its entry.
.PP
In a \fBclaim\fR record, \fBID\fR is the id of the breadcrumb the claim belongs to, and \fBLINE\fR the line of its entry. \fBTARGET\fR, \fBKIND\fR and \fBARGUMENT\fR are the three parts of the entry, without its quotes: \fBTARGET\fR is the file the claim is about, and \fBPATH\fR the file the claim is written in. \fBARGUMENT\fR may hold spaces, but no tab. \fBbcr extract\fR does not open \fBTARGET\fR: a claim about a file that does not exist is printed like any other.
.PP
Select records by their first field, and read their fields by position: new kinds of record may be added, and new fields may be added at the end of a record.
.PP
A file whose first line is not \fB\-\-\-\fR has no front matter, and prints nothing; so does front matter with no \fBbreadcrumb\fR key. A line may end in \fB\en\fR or \fB\er\en\fR.
.PP
A breadcrumb is written under the \fBbreadcrumb\fR key of the front matter, in a small part of YAML: plain one\-line values, lists of them, and \fB[]\fR. It has an \fBid\fR and a \fBtype\fR, each one word, and \fBlinks\fR, a list of entries of a verb and an id. A file whose breadcrumb breaks a rule prints no \fBbreadcrumb\fR, \fBlink\fR or \fBclaim\fR record; each problem is written to standard error, in order of line:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
PATH:LINE: MESSAGE
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each problem is also written to standard output, as a record, so that the commands after \fBbcr extract\fR in a pipe can see it:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
problem	PATH	LINE	MESSAGE
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBPATH\fR, \fBLINE\fR and \fBMESSAGE\fR are those written to standard error, with a space in place of any tab or line ending in \fBMESSAGE\fR. A file's \fBproblem\fR records come after its other records, in order of line. A \fBproblem\fR record has no id: it belongs to the breadcrumb whose record has the same \fBPATH\fR, when there is one.
.PP
A breadcrumb may also have \fBclaims\fR, a list of statements about the repository's files. \fBclaims\fR may be left out, and \fBclaims: []\fR means the same; a \fBclaims\fR with no list is a problem like any other, and the file prints no records. Each entry is written on one line, in single quotes, as \fBTARGET KIND ARGUMENT\fR, with a \fB'\fR inside it written \fB''\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
breadcrumb:
  id: verify
  type: spec
  links:
    \- follows ADR\-0016
  claims:
    \- 'docs/bcr.md has\-line ### verify'
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBTARGET\fR is a path from the root of the repository, with \fB/\fR between its parts. The only \fBKIND\fR is \fBhas\-line\fR: its \fBARGUMENT\fR is the text a line of \fBTARGET\fR must equal, once the spaces and tabs at the line's start and end are removed.
.PP
A problem in an entry of \fBclaims\fR drops only that claim: it is written to standard error, the breadcrumb's record, its links and its other claims are still printed, and the exit status is 1. The dropped claim prints no \fBclaim\fR record, only its \fBproblem\fR record. An entry has a problem when:
.IP \(bu 2
it is not in single quotes, has a comment after it on its line, or is written in any other way the breadcrumb's part of YAML does not allow, such as over several lines;
.IP \(bu 2
it has fewer than three parts, or a part is separated from the next by anything other than one space;
.IP \(bu 2
its \fBTARGET\fR starts with \fB/\fR, has a part that is \fB..\fR, or holds white space;
.IP \(bu 2
its \fBKIND\fR is not \fBhas\-line\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
its \fBhas\-line\fR text is empty, starts or ends with a space or a tab, or holds a tab.
.PP
One problem is written for each such entry.
.PP
A file or a directory that cannot be read is written to standard error as a message starting \fBbcr: \fR, and prints no record. The other files are still read.
.PP
Exit status:
.IP \(bu 2
0: every file was read, and none had a problem.
.IP \(bu 2
1: a file had a problem in its breadcrumb, or \fB.breadcrumbs\fR had a problem.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, there is no operand and no \fB.breadcrumbs\fR, or a file could not be read, even when another file had a problem.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract
git ls\-files '*.md' | xargs bcr extract
bcr extract tasks/*.md | grep '^link' | cut \-f2,4
bcr extract | grep '^claim' | cut \-f4,5
bcr extract 2>/dev/null | grep '^problem' | cut \-f2\-
.fi
.RE
.SS "verify"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr verify
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reads the records \fBbcr extract\fR prints, checks the rules about the whole set of breadcrumbs they describe, and passes every record on:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract | bcr verify
.fi
.RE
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands: none. \fBbcr verify\fR reads no file: which files make up the set is decided by \fBbcr extract\fR, from its operands or from \fB.breadcrumbs\fR.
.PP
Standard input: the records, as \fBbcr extract\fR prints them, read to the end. A line may end in \fB\en\fR or \fB\er\en\fR. A record of a kind other than \fBbreadcrumb\fR and \fBlink\fR is not checked, and neither are fields after the ones \fBbcr extract\fR prints today; both are still copied. With no input, there is no problem.
.PP
Output: standard input, copied to standard output byte for byte. Every line comes out in the order read, with its line ending as read, records of any kind included, so a later command in the pipe gets what \fBbcr extract\fR printed. \fBbcr verify\fR reads its whole input before it writes. It copies the input even when it finds a problem, and prints nothing to standard output when a line is not a record. To see only the problems, send standard output to \fB/dev/null\fR.
.PP
Each problem is written to standard error, in order of \fBPATH\fR compared as bytes, and then of \fBLINE\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
PATH:LINE: MESSAGE
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBPATH\fR and \fBLINE\fR are the ones of the record the problem is about. What is a problem:
.IP \(bu 2
Two or more breadcrumbs with the same id, or with ids that differ only in letter case, such as \fBADR\-0001\fR and \fBadr\-0001\fR. Each of them is a problem, at its \fBbreadcrumb\fR record, and its message names where the others are. Case is compared with Unicode simple case folding.
.IP \(bu 2
A link whose \fBOBJECT\fR is not the id of any breadcrumb in the set, at its \fBlink\fR record. When \fBOBJECT\fR differs only in case from an id, the message names that id.
.PP
A link to an id that two breadcrumbs share is not a problem of its own; the duplicate is.
.PP
A line of input that is not a record \fBbcr extract\fR could print, an empty line included, stops \fBbcr verify\fR: it writes a message starting \fBbcr: \fR, which gives the line's number in the input, and prints no problem.
.PP
A breadcrumb \fBbcr extract\fR could not read has no record, so links to its id are reported as pointing to no breadcrumb. Fix the problem \fBbcr extract\fR printed first.
.PP
Exit status:
.IP \(bu 2
0: no problem was found.
.IP \(bu 2
1: at least one problem was found.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr verify\fR was given an operand, its input could not be read, a line of its input was not a record, or its output could not be written.
.PP
In a pipe, the shell returns the exit status of \fBbcr verify\fR, so a problem \fBbcr extract\fR found is seen only on standard error. A script that needs both runs with \fBset \-o pipefail\fR, or saves the records first.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract | bcr verify > /dev/null
set \-o pipefail; bcr extract | bcr verify > /dev/null
bcr extract > records.tsv && bcr verify < records.tsv | cmp \- records.tsv
.fi
.RE
.SS "verdict"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr verdict [ID|PATH|\-]...
.fi
.RE
.PP
Prints the verdict of each breadcrumb (RQ\-0024), or of the breadcrumbs it is given (RQ\-0025).
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands: breadcrumbs, as DESCRIPTION defines, in the order given (RQ\-0025).
.PP
Standard input: read when an operand is \fB\-\fR. The output of any command here can feed it.
.PP
Output: one line on standard output for each breadcrumb: its id, a tab, and its verdict, \fBConfirmed\fR, \fBRefuted\fR or \fBUndecided\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
TK\-0001	Confirmed
.fi
.RE
.PP
How a verdict is reached: see audit\-report.
.PP
Exit status (RQ\-0026):
.IP \(bu 2
0: no breadcrumb printed is Refuted. Undecided ones do not change it.
.IP \(bu 2
1: a breadcrumb printed is Refuted.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, an operand names no breadcrumb, or there is no \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR directory in the current directory.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr verdict | grep \-w Refuted | cut \-f1
bcr verdict breadcrumbs/TK\-*.md
.fi
.RE
.SS "links"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr links [\-r] [ID|PATH|\-]...
.fi
.RE
.PP
Prints each link from a breadcrumb to its parent (RQ\-0028).
.PP
Flags:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-recursive\fR: also print the links of each parent reached, and of their parents, up to the breadcrumbs with none. Each link is printed once, the first time it is reached (RQ\-0030).
.PP
Operands: breadcrumbs, as DESCRIPTION defines. Only the links they have are printed, in the order given (RQ\-0029).
.PP
Standard input: read when an operand is \fB\-\fR.
.PP
Output: one line on standard output for each \fBParent:\fR link: the id of the breadcrumb that has it, a tab, and the id of the parent, in order of the breadcrumb's \fBParent:\fR lines. A link to no breadcrumb prints the path it points to in place of the parent's id; \fBbcr check\fR reports it as a problem. How a breadcrumb names its parents: TD\-0005.
.PP
Exit status (RQ\-0029):
.IP \(bu 2
0: the links were printed.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, an operand names no breadcrumb, or there is no \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR directory in the current directory.
.PP
Examples: what a Refuted Task breaks:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr verdict | grep \-w Refuted | bcr links \-r \- | cut \-f2 | sort \-u
.fi
.RE
.SS "new"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr new TYPE TITLE [PARENT|\-]...
.fi
.RE
.PP
Creates a breadcrumb in \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR and prints its path (RQ\-0031).
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTYPE\fR: \fBIN\fR, \fBRQ\fR, \fBTD\fR or \fBTK\fR, the prefix of the breadcrumb's kind (TD\-0027).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTITLE\fR: its title, on one line. Quote it in the shell.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPARENT\fR: each breadcrumb it derives from, as DESCRIPTION defines; with none, it has no parent (RQ\-0033).
.PP
Standard input: read when a \fBPARENT\fR is \fB\-\fR.
.PP
Output: the path of the new breadcrumb, relative to the repository root, on standard output. Its number is one more than the highest of its type (TD\-0027). It holds its title line, a \fBParent:\fR line for each parent, and, for a Technical Decision or a Task, the empty sections to fill in (RQ\-0032).
.PP
A breadcrumb made by \fBbcr new\fR is a draft. Who approves it before it is committed: TD\-0002 and TD\-0006.
.PP
Exit status (RQ\-0033):
.IP \(bu 2
0: the breadcrumb was written.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, the type or title is not valid, a parent names no breadcrumb, no number is left, the file could not be written, or there is no \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR directory in the current directory. Nothing is written, and no file is ever replaced.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr new TD "Use a cache" IN\-0003
bcr verdict | grep \-w Undecided | grep '^RQ' | bcr new TK "Carry them out" \-
.fi
.RE
.SS "spec"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr spec
.fi
.RE
.PP
Prints the specification of Breadcrumb SDD that this \fBbcr\fR carries out (RQ\-0035). The specification is built into the binary, so \fBbcr spec\fR reads no file and runs anywhere (TD\-0029).
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands: none.
.PP
Standard input: not read.
.PP
Output: the specification, as Markdown, on standard output. Its third line is its version, \fBVersion MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH\fR (RQ\-0036); how the version changes: TD\-0030.
.PP
Exit status:
.IP \(bu 2
0: the specification was printed.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, or standard output could not be written.
.PP
Example, the version of the specification this \fBbcr\fR carries out:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr spec | sed \-n 3p
.fi
.RE
.SS "init"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr init [\-a FILE]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sets up Breadcrumb SDD in the repository whose root is the current directory (RQ\-0042). Each piece is set up only when it is not there yet, found by its name (TD\-0034):
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbreadcrumbs/TD\-0001.md\fR, a Technical Decision to adopt the version of the specification \fBbcr spec\fR prints, written only when \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR holds no breadcrumb;
.IP \(bu 2
a \fB## Breadcrumb SDD\fR section in the agents file, pointing agents to the breadcrumbs and to that version's page on the site (RQ\-0043);
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.github/workflows/breadcrumbs.yml\fR, a GitHub Actions workflow that runs \fBbcr check\fR and \fBbcr verdict\fR, with this release of \fBbcr\fR, on each push and pull request (RQ\-0044, TD\-0035).
.PP
Flags:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-a FILE\fR, \fB\-\-agents\-file FILE\fR: the agents file, \fBAGENTS.md\fR or \fBCLAUDE.md\fR. Without it, \fBAGENTS.md\fR (RQ\-0046).
.PP
Operands: none.
.PP
Standard input: not read.
.PP
Output: on standard output, the path of each file created or added to, relative to the repository root, one per line. On standard error, a note for each piece that was already there, and left as it is.
.PP
\fBbcr init\fR replaces no file, and changes none but the agents file, by adding its section at the end (RQ\-0045). It needs a released \fBbcr\fR, since the workflow installs the same release (TD\-0036).
.PP
On a CI other than GitHub Actions, the same check is:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr check && bcr verdict
.fi
.RE
.PP
Exit status (RQ\-0045):
.IP \(bu 2
0: everything is set up, including when it already was.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, has no version, or a piece cannot be set up. Nothing is written when this is found before writing.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr init
bcr init \-a CLAUDE.md
.fi
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
Every command exits with (RQ\-0010):
.IP \(bu 2
0: it succeeded and found nothing wrong.
.IP \(bu 2
1: it ran and found something wrong in the breadcrumbs.
.IP \(bu 2
2: it was used wrongly, or could not run.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
The method: \fBREADME.md\fR. Its rules: the specification, printed by \fBbcr spec\fR, and the breadcrumbs in \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR. How the repository is developed: \fBCONTRIBUTING.md\fR.
